    <h1>Shipping packaging for peptides</h1>
    <p class="lead muted">Packaging has one job: deliver the vial in the condition it left the factory. Everything else — branding, inserts, unboxing — is secondary to mechanical protection, moisture control and temperature stability.</p>
    <p class="small muted" style="margin-bottom:18px;">Part of the <a href="/knowledge/peptide-shipping-customs-framework/">shipping &amp; customs pillar</a>.</p>

    <h2>Layer by layer</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Function</th><th>Practical note</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Primary</td><td>Vial, stopper, crimp</td><td>Seal integrity is the first check on arrival</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cushioning</td><td>Absorb shock and vibration</td><td>Individual vial pockets beat loose fill</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Moisture control</td><td>Desiccant in a sealed barrier</td><td>Protects the cake if the outer pack is breached</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Temperature</td><td>Phase-change packs where needed</td><td>Size the pack for transit time, not for the handover</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Outer</td><td>Rigid box, tamper evidence</td><td>Label must match the customs documents</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Temperature: match the pack to the route</h2>
    <p>Lyophilized peptides tolerate short ambient exposure, but a multi-day summer route or a customs hold can exceed that tolerance. Use phase-change packs for hot routes and insulated liners for multi-day transit; for sensitive compounds, a cold-chain carrier is the honest option rather than a generic express service with an ice pack.</p>

    <h2>What to check on arrival</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Outer packaging intact; no crush, water damage or resealing.</li>
      <li>Phase-change packs still within their working range, if used.</li>
      <li>Vial seals and crimps intact; no loose stopper.</li>
      <li>Label batch number matches the COA and invoice.</li>
      <li>Cake appearance consistent with the certificate.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Label consistency</h2>
    <p>The label is part of the customs file. Product name, strength, batch and quantity should match the invoice and packing list. A label that disagrees with the documents is an invitation to inspect.</p>


    <h2>A simple packaging qualification test</h2>
    <p>Before shipping a new configuration, run a drop and dwell test with a dummy load of the same weight and geometry:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop the packed box from the carrier's stated handling height onto a hard surface — six faces, three edges, one corner.</li>
      <li>Inspect vials for cracks, stopper movement and label damage.</li>
      <li>Hold the pack at the expected transit temperature for the expected duration and check that phase-change packs remain within range.</li>
      <li>Open the pack and check for condensation on the vials.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>This is not a substitute for a validated shipping study, but it catches the common failures — insufficient cushioning, undersized phase-change packs, and desiccant that is saturated before departure.</p>

    <h2>Cost vs protection</h2>
    <p>Packaging cost scales with protection, but the relationship is not linear. The largest gains usually come from three cheap changes: individual vial pockets instead of loose fill, a sealed moisture barrier with fresh desiccant, and a box sized to the contents rather than a standard carton with empty space. Temperature control is the expensive layer — add it where the route or the compound justifies it, not by default.</p>
    <p>Measure the trade-off in expected loss, not in packaging spend: a shipment that arrives warm may be recoverable; one that arrives cracked is not.</p>

    <h2>Editor's take</h2>
    <p>Packaging is the only part of quality control the customer physically receives. If the vial arrives intact and the label matches the file, the chain worked; if not, nothing else matters.</p>

